@rfcbot reviewed

While the `addr_of!` and `addr_of_mut!` macros were a good stopgap, raw pointers are important enough in Rust that they seem to justify having a reference-style primitive constructor syntax.

I re-read RFC 2582 and the marathon [thread](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/2582) for it after RalfJ posted this stabilization, and the outcome that was arrived at seemed sound and well motivated.  Every angle that came to my mind seemed to have been considered.  I'm comfortable there's not a lot more waiting to be discovered in the design space here.

I wasn't a part of the original RFC thread, but I tend to agree that a more dedicated syntax that doesn't include the word `addr` would be nice to have.

The prefix syntax often isn't great generally, but this will make raw pointers feel more integrated into, and supported by, the language. At the very least that's a subjective difference that I think is worth supporting.

@rfcbot reviewed

TBH, I'm kinda torn here because I've kinda liked the grouping that the macro provides, and I end up wanting to call methods on it often if I'm using a raw pointer -- like `addr_of!(x).cast()` -- both of which are kinda elegant with the macro.  Not to mention that I continue to wish we had a better raw pointer type, say one that can have LLVM alignment parameter attributes.  But I agree that having "native" syntax would be nice, and agree that "addr" is a bit of an unfortunate name.

But after meeting conversation I'm convinced today that we might as well just go forward, and the specific `raw mut` and `raw const` syntax means we can always find some other thing for other things if needed for something else in future.
