I ran into an issue where it appears that naked (const) generic functions get incorrectly inlined at some earlier point

```rust
//@ only-x86_64

#![crate_type = "lib"]
#![feature(naked_functions, asm_const, linkage)]

#[no_mangle]
fn test_const_generics(x: u64) -> u64 {
    using_const_generics::<1>(x);
    using_const_generics::<2>(x)
}

// CHECK-LABEL: using_const_generics

#[naked]
pub extern "C" fn using_const_generics<const N: u64>(x: u64) -> u64 {
    const M: u64 = 42;

    unsafe {
        core::arch::asm!(
            "xor rax, rax",
            "add rax, rdi",
            "add rax, {}",
            "add rax, {}",
            "ret",
            const N,
            const M,
            options(noreturn),
        )
    }
}
```

somehow still generates LLVM with an inline asm block

```llvm
; Function Attrs: nonlazybind uwtable
define noundef i64 @test_const_generics(i64 noundef %x) unnamed_addr #0 {
start:
  call void asm sideeffect alignstack inteldialect "xor rax, rax\0Aadd rax, rdi\0Aadd rax, 1\0Aadd rax, 42\0Aret", "~{dirflag},~{fpsr},~{flags},~{memory}"(), !srcloc !3
  unreachable
}
```

I think what's happening here is that at some earlier point the `using_const_generics` function is inlined, and because the asm block is marked as `noreturn`, the second call to `using_const_generics` is just dropped.

I tried setting `codegen_fn_attrs.inline = InlineAttr::Never` in `compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/codegen_attrs.rs` but that is somehow not enough to guarantee that this generic funciont doesn't get inlined?! Weirdly, removing the `#[naked]` and replacing it with `#[inline(never)]` does generate the expected code, with 2 specializations of the generic function (this is weird because I think setting `codegen_fn_attrs.inline = InlineAttr::Never` should have the same effect as a `#[inline(never)]` in the source code). Also, this is specific somehow to (const) generics: using plain functions does not run into this issue.

edit: turns out inlining got cleared later on in the function where I set it :facepalm: all good now.

Yes, the build fails for the `x86_64-gnu-nopt` configuration, because this regex does not match:

```rust
// CHECK: declare x86_intrcc void @page_fault_handler(ptr {{.*}}, i64 {{.*}}){{.*}}#[[ATTRS:[0-9]+]]
// CHECK-NOT: memcpy
#[naked]
#[no_mangle]
pub extern "x86-interrupt" fn page_fault_handler(_: u64, _: u64) {
    unsafe {
        core::arch::naked_asm!("ud2");
    }
}
```

we get these declarations

```
; opt level 0
declare x86_intrcc void @page_fault_handler(ptr byval([8 x i8]) align 8, i64) unnamed_addr #1
; opt level != 0
declare x86_intrcc void @page_fault_handler(ptr noalias nocapture noundef byval([8 x i8]) align 8 dereferenceable(8), i64 noundef) unnamed_addr #1
```

The space after `i64` in the original regex made the regex not match for opt level 0. Removing the space fixes the issue.

